Because variables live only in RAM (memory) while the program is running. If you want data to remain after the program closes, you need files.

A file is permanent storage on your computer.
CRUD operations:
    Create
    Read
    Update
    Delete

Opening a File - open(filename, mode)
If the file already exists, Python raises:  FileExistsError

File Modes:
"r" -	Read
"w" -	Write (overwrite)
"a" -	Append
"x"	- Create new file
"rb" -	Read binary
"wb" -	Write binary

Writing to a File  - file.write("...")
Closing File - file.close()

Why close()?  -  When Python opens a file, it reserves system resources. Always close it when you're finished.

Reading a File -- file.read()
Readling one line -- file.readline()
Reading All Lines  - file.readlines()

Using .strip() removes the newline character.

Appending to a File --- Write mode ("w") replaces everything,  If you want to keep the existing content, use append mode.

The Better Way to open a file: with
    with open("notes.txt", "r") as file:
        print(file.read())

When the with block ends, Python automatically closes the file. This is the recommended approach in modern Python.


File Paths:
    open("/home/harina/Documents/notes.txt") -- in other os
    open("C:\\Users\\Harina\\Documents\\notes.txt")  -- in windows
    open(r"C:\Users\Harina\Documents\notes.txt")  -- The r creates a raw string, so backslashes don't need escaping.

--- Quick Revision Notes ---
Files are used to store data permanently, even after the program ends.
You can create, read, update, and delete files.

Common methods:
- open() → opens a file
- read() → reads the whole file
- readline() → reads one line
- readlines() → reads all lines as a list
- write() → writes text to a file
- close() → closes the file

Best practice:
Use with open(...) as file: so the file closes automatically.

Example:
with open("notes.txt", "a") as file:
    file.write("Hello\n")

Important points:
- "w" will erase old content and replace it
- "a" keeps the existing content and adds new content
- .strip() removes extra spaces and newline characters

Remember:
File handling helps you save user input, store records, and read data later.

